## CLASSIFICATION CRITERIA

complexity = "trivial" when the goal is a SINGLE self-contained deliverable an agent can produce in ONE writing pass with no external research, no new specialist, and no multi-step plan:
- "a list of 10 birds", "a haiku about the sea", "a one-page markdown summary of X"
- "a sample JSON config", "a short outline", "rename these three things"
- "a detailed 2-3 page essay on photosynthesis" (long-ish but still one pass)
- Anything where the answer IS the deliverable and its FULL text fits in one writing pass (roughly up to ~2,000 words of output).

complexity = "moderate" when the goal needs a few clear steps or one existing specialist, but no research, no training, and no multi-phase program -- INCLUDING a conceptually-simple deliverable whose full text is too large for one writing pass and must be produced section by section:
- "compare these two documents and write up the differences"
- "draft a project plan from this brief"
- "summarize across the files already in my knowledge base"
- "a file with 10 folk tales, each told as a COMPLETE story with all scenes and dialogue" (one deliverable, but ~10 sections of full prose -- far beyond one pass)
- "a 30-page handbook", "full chapter drafts for each item in this outline"

complexity = "complex" when the goal is a multi-phase program, needs multiple specialists, requires external research/web fetches, or implies building a durable reused capability:
- "build me a competitive-analysis report researching the top 5 vendors"
- "set up an ongoing weekly digest of my calendar and email"
- "create a specialist that can do X going forward"

## RULES
- When in doubt between trivial and moderate, prefer the CHEAPER class (trivial) -- a misclassified trivial goal that turns out to need more simply produces a first pass; a misclassified moderate goal needlessly burns the expensive loop. EXCEPTION: when the doubt is about OUTPUT VOLUME, prefer moderate -- a too-large deliverable forced into one pass doesn't degrade gracefully, it times out and the user gets nothing.
- Estimate the VOLUME of the finished deliverable, not how hard it is to think about. A simple-sounding goal that requires emitting many sections of full prose ("N complete stories/chapters/profiles, each written out in full") exceeds one writing pass and is AT LEAST moderate. Rough budget: one writing pass produces ~1,500-2,000 words; beyond that the work must be split into per-section tasks.
- Qualifiers like "full", "complete", "in full detail", "every scene", "not a summary" multiply the per-item volume -- weigh them.
- Mentioning a tool the agent already has (write a file, make a list) is NOT complexity.

## SECTIONABLE (a deliverable cut into ordered sections)

Set `sectionable: true` when the deliverable decomposes into clear, bounded SECTIONS, produced one after another in the order you list them and then assembled into the final result. List one `sections` entry per section, in order, and give a one-line `assembly`. Research-backed documents can use this path too: evidence gathering and drafting are separate, bounded sections with explicit dependencies.

SIZE EACH STEP, NOT JUST THE WHOLE DOCUMENT. An execution step has a limited
model-call window. A heading such as "All profiles", "Research everything" or
"Write the entire report" is not a decomposition of a large task. Split a
large set into named, non-overlapping batches with explicit coverage boundaries.
Aim for about 500-800 words of new prose per drafting section, with at most
24 sections in this plan. Preserve the requested depth and ALL requested items
across the batches; do not shorten the whole deliverable to fit one call. If
24 bounded sections cannot cover it, use the normal multi-phase planner.
Research sections return compact evidence with source URLs, supported facts and
gaps; they do not also write the complete report. Drafting sections consume
only their relevant evidence. An assembly combines finished sections and must
not rewrite the entire document in another giant writing pass.

For a document covering dozens of people, products or historical events, use
several small, named batches in the requested order, with bounded evidence
gathering feeding each batch. Do not put every biography in a single step and
then ask a second step to write them all again. Verify the requested coverage
from research instead of guessing a total or silently omitting later items.

Two shapes qualify:
- INDEPENDENT sections -- the "N complete things, each written out in full" pattern. Each section is producible on its own, WITHOUT the output of any other section, so their order does not matter; their `inputs` name only the goal's own inputs.
  - "a file with 10 folk tales, each a COMPLETE story" -> sectionable: true; one section per tale; assembly = "concatenate the ten stories into one markdown file".
  - "full chapter drafts for each item in this outline" -> sectionable: true; one section per chapter.
  - "a profile of each of these 6 vendors" -> sectionable: true; one section per vendor.
- ORDERED sections -- a few clear steps where a later section reads what an earlier one produced. The later section names that output in its `inputs`, spelled exactly as the earlier section spelled it in its `outputs`.
  - "summarise last month's invoices and draft an email to finance with the totals" -> sectionable: true; section 1 "summarise the month's invoices" has outputs ["invoiceSummary"]; section 2 "draft the email to finance" has inputs ["invoiceSummary"].
  - Read an external request, make the requested change, then verify and publish it: the read returns observed requirements and applicable instructions; the change consumes them and declares its effects; verification and publication consume the original requirements and applicable instructions alongside the change receipts. Do not preselect the implementation while classifying. Add a final saved report only when the person asks for one.

Every section entry carries:
- `label`: a short name for the section.
- `instruction`: what to produce for THIS section.
- `purpose`: the section as a goal of its own, in one line, in the words a person would use to ask for just this part ("summarise the month's invoices"). Keep it general rather than tied to this goal's specifics: earlier work that did the same thing is found by it, and reused instead of planned again.
- `inputs`: the names this section reads -- the goal's own input names (as the goal names them), or an earlier section's outputs. `[]` when it reads nothing by name.
- `outputs`: the names of what this section produces, as single words ("invoiceSummary", "chapter3").
- `reuseIntent`: "reusable" when the section is a general capability other goals would want too, "goalSpecific" when it only makes sense for this goal, "accountSpecific" when it depends on one client's or account's specifics.
- `effects`: what the section CHANGES besides producing text -- "files" (writes or edits files), "machine" (acts on the person's computer), "external" (sends, posts or calls anything outside, such as an email, a webhook or an API), "spend" (costs money), or "concept:<v1 concept id>" (writes records of that kind, for example "concept:v1:todos:todo"). `[]` for a section that only reads and writes text.
- Intermediate research notes and document drafts have `effects: []`: the final file is saved by the separate assembly step. The fact that the overall goal requests a file does not turn each draft into a file write. Do not add a section that saves the same final file a second time.
- `postcondition`: how the END of the section is checked -- "the report file exists in the Library", "a v1:todos:todo with that title exists". REQUIRED whenever `effects` is not empty; `""` for a section with no effects, whose outputs are its end.

THE BOUNDARY RULE: every section must end at a point that can be checked. A section that changes something says how its end is checked (a `postcondition`), and a section that changes nothing names what it produces (at least one `output`). A decomposition with a section that would stop in the middle of a change -- `effects` without a `postcondition` -- is refused, and the goal is then planned as a whole instead, which costs more.

For a read or prerequisite section, define an observable handoff: supported
requirements, applicable constraints, source references and explicit gaps needed
by the next section. "Understood" is not a checkable output. Bound the section
to those requirements, not a survey of every linked source. Preserve required
instruction coverage and identify any missing passage; later implementation or
verification belongs to its own section. A drafting section still returns its
complete requested content, never just a summary or a progress note.

Earlier outputs reach a section only through named inputs; an earlier step's
presence in the plan does not supply its contents. Name the original requirements and applicable
instructions as inputs to every section that must obey or check them, alongside
the relevant result receipts. Do not replace these criteria with a previous
section's claim that its work is done.

Assign every requested acceptance criterion to a section. When criteria await
a bounded prerequisite read, make later sections responsible for its applicable
requirements instead of guessing them. State how each
section checks its applicable criteria and hands off unmet criteria and later
obligations with their source references. Keep later obligations separate from
the current section's completion condition. When verification is required,
creating required verification and running existing checks are different obligations;
assign both when the request requires both. A passing check or a publication
receipt alone does not establish that every requested criterion was satisfied.

Set `sectionable: false` (the default) when:
- the work cannot be divided into independently checkable outputs, even with explicit dependencies,
- a section could only end in the middle of a change (the boundary rule above),
- it's `trivial` (fits in one pass -- no sections needed), or
- it needs a multi-phase program or multiple specialists that cannot be represented by these bounded sections. Complexity alone does not prohibit sectioning a research document.

When in doubt, prefer `sectionable: false` -- a non-sectionable goal simply routes through the normal planner; a wrongly-sectioned one splits work that was not actually separable.

